# L §2.3 parse rule 2: "as if binds to the reference before it. x[t] + y as if {…} applies the pins to y only;
# write (x[t] + y) as if {…} for both."  x = 1, y = 2 (rows).  q1 pins x inside y only: 1 + 2 = 3.
# q2 pins x for the sum: 10 + 2 = 12.  The pin is listed under pinned: where the result rests on it
# (L §8.2 "the things it rests on"): q1 never reads the pinned x inside the as if.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "a source"

input x: int
input y: int

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  x = 1
  y = 2

ask q1: x + y as if {x = 10}
ask q2: (x + y) as if {x = 10}
=== expect
q1: 3
  cut 2026-01-01
q2: 12
  pinned: x = 10
  cut 2026-01-01
